@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About</PageTitle>

<h2>About this app</h2>

<p class="lede">
    A Blazor web app that uses only static server rendering: no interactive server render mode, no
    WebAssembly, no prerendering, and no Blazor client boot script. The only client script is htmx, and
    only for fragment swaps. It consumes
    <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/DataMapper.Extensions">DataMapper.Extensions 1.0.0</a> from
    NuGet rather than the source in this repo, so it is an ordinary consumer of the package.
</p>

<h3>What that means for the UI</h3>

<p>
    With no circuit there are no component events, so nothing in this app can respond to a click in the
    Blazor sense. Filters travel in the query string through plain GET forms, and the actions post to
    minimal API endpoints. Every page is one render of one component per request.
</p>

<p>
    htmx is used where a full page reload would otherwise be the only option: the jobs filter and pager
    swap just the results region, the streaming page appends one chunk at a time as each chunk is
    revealed, and the archive replay swaps the archive region. Those endpoints return a fragment through
    <code>RazorComponentResult</code>. A swap does not move the scroll position, which is the difference
    between htmx and a reload, and the endpoints send <code>HX-Push-Url</code> with the page URL so the
    address bar still records what was run. Where htmx does not fit it is not used: a CSV download cannot
    be swapped because the response is a file rather than markup, and dark mode is a browser preference
    rather than something a fragment can change. Every htmx element keeps a plain <code>href</code> or
    form action, so the app still works with the script disabled.
</p>

